<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  A sterling sign inside a circle, matching the iOS app's "sterlingsign.circle" SF Symbol on the
  same control (fuel-ios: NearbyView.swift / CheapestToggleTip.swift). Material Icons ships no
  pound-in-circle (MonetizationOn, used here before, is a dollar), so this is hand-assembled:

  - The ring is a filled annulus (outer r=10, inner r=8.2 about the 24x24 viewport's centre),
    with android:fillType="evenOdd" carving the hole out — evenOdd rather than two
    opposite-winding subpaths (which is how Material's own generated icons do it) because it
    carves the hole whichever way the two arcs happen to wind. A fill rather than a stroked
    circle to stay consistent with Material's fill-only icons; Compose tints strokes perfectly
    well, so that part is convention, not a constraint.
  - The glyph is the U+00A3 outline from the bundled DM Mono Medium — the same brand font as the
    "fuel tracker.uk" wordmark (BrandTitle) and the logo's own pound sign — scaled to 11.2dp
    tall and centred, so the icon reads as the app's typeface rather than a traced approximation.
    Regenerate with fontTools rather than editing the path data by hand.

  No android:tint here: Compose's Icon supplies the tint (LocalContentColor) as a ColorFilter over
  the whole vector, so these #FF000000 fills get replaced wholesale in either theme. Any reuse
  outside Compose — an ImageView, or the Car App Library's IconCompat the way core's
  ic_refresh.xml does — has to tint it itself, or it renders black.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FF000000"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
